2a:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"shard"}]," distributes keys across several backends by hash, with an optional\ninteger ",["$","code",null,{"children":"weight"}]," per shard. The weights are turned into a cumulative table over\nthe ",["$","code",null,{"children":"u32"}]," space at startup and the key is binary-searched into it."]}]
2b:["$","a",null,{"href":"https://github.com/toxicwind/forge/blob/v1.6.5/forge-store/src/shard_store.rs","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","data-source-link":"forge-store/src/shard_store.rs","data-source-ref":"v1.6.5","title":"forge-store/src/shard_store.rs at v1.6.5","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 align-middle font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground no-underline transition-colors hover:border-brand/50 hover:text-brand","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"12","height":"12","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"m16 18 6-6-6-6M8 6l-6 6 6 6"}]}],"ShardStore"]}]
2c:["$","p",null,{"children":"Two properties decide whether it fits."}]
2d:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Placement includes the blob's size."}]," The hash folds the digest ",["$","em",null,{"children":"and"}]," its\ndeclared size, so the same content at a different declared size lands in a\ndifferent shard. This is fine (it's still deterministic), but it means you\ncannot reason about placement from the hash alone."]}]
2e:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Nothing rebalances."}]," No migration, no virtual-node consistent hashing,\nno read repair, no re-shard command. Adding, removing, or reweighting a shard\nmoves the boundaries and remaps a large fraction of keys. The data is still\nphysically where it was written; lookups just go somewhere else and never find\nit."]}]
2f:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-amber-500/40 bg-amber-50/60 text-foreground dark:bg-amber-500/10","role":"alert","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400","aria-hidden":"true","children":"$10:props:children:0:props:children"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"Treat any shard change as a cache flush"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Plan a shard-list change the way you would plan wiping the cache: during a\nquiet window, with the expectation that the next build is cold. Size the shard\ncount for where you expect to be in a year rather than for today."}]]}]]}]
30:["$","p",null,{"children":"Sharding is the right tool when one backend is genuinely the ceiling: a single\nRedis instance's memory, one filesystem's IOPS. It is the wrong tool for\ncapacity you could have bought by pointing at object storage instead."}]
